{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And the princes of Israel offered… and they brought their offering before HaShem</b> — Because the wagons served the needs of the offerings, they are called a קָרְבָּן, as in \"and we brought near HaShem's offering, every man who found a gold vessel\" (Numbers 31:50) — an offering for the upkeep of the House. Now the princes reasoned that it was not feasible for the Levites to carry on their shoulders the planks of the Tabernacle and the sockets, for they were very heavy, and so they brought wagons of their own, for such is the way of all who transport the houses of kings and their tent-palaces — to carry them in wagons. It is also possible to parse <b>and they brought their offering before HaShem: six covered wagons</b> — six large wagons carrying their offerings, and twelve cattle drawing the wagons. They brought the wagons full and the cattle before the Tabernacle, and HaShem commanded Moses, \"Take from them\" — all of them, and the wagons and the cattle that were not for an offering were to perform the service of the Tent of Meeting. Afterward the princes took their offerings off the wagons and brought them before the Tabernacle, for they intended to offer everything that very day once they were permitted to offer it to Him, but HaShem commanded, \"One prince per day they shall offer\" (Numbers 7:11). Therefore it was unnecessary to say \"Take from them\" now. It is the way of the language to use \"wagons\" for that which is loaded upon them, as in \"those who eat at Jezebel's table\" (I Kings 18:19), and similarly \"for all the gate of my people knows\" (Ruth 3:11), and the language of the Sages (Bava Batra 146a): \"a hundred barrels of jugs of wine, of jugs of oil, and of vessels of silver and gold.\" It is also possible that \"צָב\" means full ones, as in \"in wagons (וּבַצַּבִּים) and in mules\" (Isaiah 66:20) — in wagons full of people, and <b>six covered wagons</b> would mean six wagons of loads, and their loads were from the dedication offerings which he will specify. Scripture mentioned the princes' offerings in detail and then gathered them together: \"This was the dedication of the altar on the day it was anointed, from the princes of Israel: twelve silver bowls\" (Numbers 7:84). Rashi wrote (Rashi on Numbers 7:85): To teach you that the sacred vessels were precise in their weight — he would weigh them one by one and weigh them again all together, and they were neither more nor less; from the teaching of Rabbi Moses the Preacher. But I do not understand their reasoning in this matter. If we suppose it to be a miracle, what benefit is there in this thing, that such a miracle should be performed in it? And if it is the way of the whole world — that is so with all weighings — why did Scripture mention it? In the Sifrei they said explicitly: To teach you that the vessels of the Sanctuary are not like the vessels of an ordinary person. The vessels of an ordinary person — one weighs them one by one and weighs them again all together, sometimes more, sometimes less, etc. And there they said: Rabbi Nathan says: The vessels of the Sanctuary — he weighed them all, then made an ingot from them, and made them into vessels again — neither more nor less. This means to say that they were extremely refined gold, so that even if one melts them down and makes an ingot of them, they would be at their original weight, and not that a miracle was performed in them, but rather that they were very pure gold. The correct explanation of Scripture's reasoning is that the Holy One, blessed be He, accords honor to those who fear Him, as He said: \"For those who honor Me I will honor\" (I Samuel 2:30). Now all the princes on a single day brought this offering upon which they had agreed together, and it is impossible that one did not precede the other, and He honored those who came first among the standards by advancing their days. But He wished to mention each by name, with the detail of his offering, and to mention the day of each one — not that He would mention and honor only the first, \"this is the offering of Nahshon son of Amminadav,\" and say: and so the princes offered, each on his day, for that would be a curtailment of the honor of the rest. And afterward He gathered them together to indicate that they were all equal before Him, blessed be He, and so they said there in the Sifrei (Naso 160): \"Scripture informs that just as they were all equal in one counsel, so they were all equal in merit. Twelve silver bowls — they are the very ones they donated, and no disqualification occurred in them.\" And there is yet another explanation in their midrash: each one of the princes had it arise in thought to bring a dedication for the altar and that it should be in this measure, but Nahshon reasoned one rationale for this measure, while each of the other princes reasoned his own rationale. They said that Nahshon intended to bring a silver bowl whose letter-count would be nine hundred and thirty, corresponding to the years of a man's life, and its weight one hundred and thirty, corresponding to the descendants he established, and the entire midrash is as Rashi wrote it (Rashi on Numbers 7:19). Or according to the other midrash (Numbers Rabbah 13:13), there was a tradition handed down from Jacob our father to every tribe, of all that would befall it until the days of the Messiah. Nahshon began and offered according to the order of kingship: a bowl and a basin, corresponding to two kings who would issue from him, who would rule over sea and dry land — Solomon and King Messiah. Therefore he offered a bowl corresponding to the sea, which surrounds the entire world and resembles a bowl. <b>One hundred thirty</b> — for on the third day the Holy One, blessed be He, gathered all the seas into one place and called them \"ימים\" (seas), which in gematria is one hundred, and Solomon added one sea for the work of the Temple, and a line of thirty cubits would surround it (I Kings 7:23) — making one hundred and thirty. <b>One silver basin</b> — corresponding to the world, which is made like a sphere. <b>Seventy shekels</b> — for they would rule over seventy nations. <b>Both of them full</b> — that they would bring meal-offerings from all of them. <b>Fine flour</b> — \"those refined in gold\" (Lamentations 4:2). <b>Mixed with oil</b> — \"A good name is better than good oil\" (Ecclesiastes 7:1). <b>Silver</b> — as it says, \"Choice silver is the tongue of the righteous\" (Proverbs 10:20). <b>One golden pan of ten</b> — corresponding to ten generations from Perez to David, all of whom were righteous, full of good deeds like the fragrance of incense. <b>One bull</b> — corresponding to Abraham. <b>One ram</b> — corresponding to Isaac. <b>One lamb</b> — corresponding to Jacob. <b>One goat</b> — to atone for the deed of Judah, who brought the tunic to his father. <b>And for the sacrifice of peace-offerings, two cattle</b> — corresponding to David and Solomon, who initiated the kingship and were righteous, and the kingship was whole. <b>Five rams, five goats, five lambs of the first year</b> — corresponding to fifteen kings from Rehoboam to Zedekiah, a king the son of a king — some perfectly righteous, some intermediate, some perfectly wicked. This is the thought of Nahshon son of Amminadav. Netanel son of Zuar also reasoned in his heart to bring a dedication in this measure, and he reasoned a different rationale for it (Numbers Rabbah 13:15), and offered in the name of the Torah, for the praise of the tribe of Issachar was wisdom in Torah: a silver bowl — corresponding to the Torah, which is called bread, as it says, \"Come, eat of my bread\" (Proverbs 9:5), and it says regarding the showbread (Exodus 25:29), \"you shall make its bowls.\" Zebulun offered, for he was engaged in commerce, toiling and placing food into the mouth of Issachar, receiving reward with him: a bowl — corresponding to the sea, for he was on the seacoast (Numbers Rabbah 13:16). And so they found there in the midrash a special rationale for every tribe in its offering and in the measures of the offering. Therefore Scripture set them out in detail, each one individually, as though the other had not been mentioned, and afterward gathered them as one, to hint that at one and the same time it arose in their thought to bring the dedication, and no one preceded the other in thought or in bringing before the Tabernacle — and for this reason Scripture mentioned them all in equality:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "Ramban references Bava Batra 146a for the idiom of 'barrels of jugs'; the page reference follows standard Vilna folio citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:14.785520203Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}